uam's Maxwell target (TargetGM107::canDualIssue): no dual issue.

uam pairs an ALU instruction with a following load or SFU instruction
(delay 0); on the console that left later instructions reading registers
their producers had not written yet (the GX lighting's light term read as
zero, a colour channel black), while emulators, which ignore the
scheduling words, drew it right. Mesa's own GM107 target never dual-issues
(canDualIssue returns false), and its code runs right on the console.

diff --git a/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir_target_gm107.cpp b/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir_target_gm107.cpp
index 50d0e80..ad1206c 100644
--- a/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir_target_gm107.cpp
+++ b/mesa-imported/codegen/nv50_ir_target_gm107.cpp
@@ -291,6 +291,13 @@ getDualIssueClass(const Instruction *insn)
 bool
 TargetGM107::canDualIssue(const Instruction *a, const Instruction *b) const
 {
+   // Never: on the console, pairs this allowed (an ALU instruction with the
+   // load or SFU instruction after it) left later instructions reading
+   // registers that had not been written (the GX lighting's light term came
+   // out zero, and a channel black); Mesa's own GM107 scheduler never
+   // dual-issues either. Emulators ignore the scheduling words.
+   return false;
+
    if (!a->canCommuteDefDef(b) || !a->canCommuteDefSrc(b))
       return false;
 
